{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a thought-provoking film that delves into the world of social media and the consequences of navigating the complex landscape of friendship, ambition, and betrayal. directed by david fincher and written by grant heslov, benjamin meadow, and scott zar, this movie tells the story of mark Zuckerberg, a brilliant but reclusive college student who creates afacebook, which eventually becomes a global phenomenon.\n\nthe film stars jensen ackles as eric, a high school student and mark's best friend, and ty burrell as divya, a business partner who helps mark launch facebook. however, the main focus of the movie is on jared leto, who plays mark zuckerberg, and armie hammer, who portrays dylan hall, a fellow hacker and friend.\n\none of the standout aspects of the movie is its portrayal of the human side of mark zuckerberg. through jared leto's performance, the film humanizes the enigmatic figure of mark zuckerberg, revealing the emotional turmoil and vulnerability that lies beneath his surface. armie hammer's character, dylan hall, serves as a foil to mark, highlighting the tension and competition that develops as their friendship turns into a bitter rivalry.\n\nthe film's portrayal of thefacebook's early days is also impressive. the movie shows the dedication and perseverance required to launch a successful startup, as well as the challenges that come with navigating the complex world of campus politics and social dynamics.\n\nthe film's script is sharp and witty, with clever dialogue that captures the essence of the characters and their relationships. the cinematography is also noteworthy, with a muted color palette that effectively conveys the mood and atmosphere of the story.\n\none of the most striking aspects of the movie is its commentary on the consequences of success. as mark zuckerberg's facebook becomes a global phenomenon, he fi